Revision history for File-SOPS

{{$NEXT}}
  - Multi-document YAML is now supported, read and written. decrypt returns an
    ArrayRef of HashRefs for a stream that really holds more than one document
    (a single document stays a bare HashRef); encrypt, encrypt_file,
    encrypt_in_place and decrypt_file all handle a stream, with one metadata
    section written byte-identically into every document and one MAC over every
    document in order. Metadata is read from the first document, as sops does; a
    stream carrying it only in a later document is refused. extract gains a
    document => $n argument (default 0) to reach a chosen document, and names
    which document a not-found path was searched in. Converting a stream to a
    format that has no document stream (JSON, ENV, INI) is refused, naming the
    count and the target, rather than silently dropping documents as sops does.
    edit on a stream is deliberately not enabled yet (karr #41). Single-document
    files are byte-identical. (docs/adr/0033, karr #31)
  - ARBITRATION: a caller-supplied integer SV in the int64..uint64 window (a
    Perl UV literal, a computed integer, or a scalar out of a YAML parse -- not
    one read through Format::JSON::parse, which docs/adr/0021 fixed) is refused
    by assert_representable; accepted as a limit rather than closed by loosening
    a MAC-path guard. The croak already names both workarounds (store it as a
    string, or as a float). No code change. (docs/adr/0069, docs/adr/0021, karr
    #104)
  - decrypt now carps when a plaintext comment stands in a slot the encryption
    rule marks as encrypted, matching sops' "Found possibly unencrypted comment
    in file" warning. Advisory only: the comment is kept, the MAC and value do
    not move, and the comment text is never quoted. Reaches dotenv and ini;
    yaml cannot reach it (YAML::XS discards plaintext comments before parse).
    (docs/adr/0067, karr #173)
  - File::SOPS::Comment is now a Moo class like every other class here,
    reversing ADR 0041's hand-written bless. Its three constructor refusals
    (missing text, a reference, an empty string) are unchanged, and no wire
    byte moves. (docs/adr/0068, karr #154)
  - SECURITY FIX and BEHAVIOUR CHANGE: unencrypted_regex, encrypted_regex and
    a .sops.yaml path_regex are matched in Go RE2's dialect (compiled /a), not
    Perl's, so a rule selects the same keys here as at sops -- Perl's
    Unicode-aware \w/\d/\s over a non-ASCII key used to leave readable a value
    sops encrypts. A pattern the two dialects read differently, or that Perl
    cannot compile, is refused (naming the rule, once, before any leaf); a
    pattern RE2 cannot compile matches nothing on the READ path as sops does,
    and is refused only for writing. An ASCII rule over ASCII keys does not
    move. decrypt and extract are unaffected by a write-side refusal. (docs/adr/0048,
    docs/adr/0051, karr #161, #164, #166, #171)
  - ARBITRATION: the residual RE2/Perl regex divergences are accepted as limits
    rather than closed -- Perl full case folding (`(?i)^ss$` over U+00DF), `$`
    before a trailing newline, `\p{Word}`, and a pattern neither dialect can
    compile (`fo(`, read by sops at exit 0, refused here). None is reachable by
    an ASCII rule over ASCII keys, and each fails loudly. No code change.
    (docs/adr/0065, docs/adr/0066, karr #165, #176)
  - PERFORMANCE: should_encrypt_path reads each rule attribute once and caches
    the suffix matcher, about 10% off the common single-suffix call. No
    behaviour change. (karr #142)
  - ARBITRATION: the JSON surrogate-pair divergence (Cpanel::JSON::XS combines
    the pair, YAML::PP keeps the two lone surrogates) is accepted as a limit;
    the JSON reparse already refuses such a leaf loudly, naming its path, and
    only a hand-written or third-party JSON document reaches it. No wire byte
    moves. (docs/adr/0064, karr #139)
  - NEW DIAGNOSTIC: a MAC verification failure now appends a hedged hint for two
    shapes sops writes but then cannot read back -- a bare `-0`/`-0.0` token in a
    YAML/JSON unencrypted slot (a float that underflowed to negative zero), and
    an env/ini unencrypted value written in a Go display form (`true`/`<nil>`/
    `N.0`/exponent). The hint names the leaf and points at ignore_mac; it fires
    only on MAC failure, is phrased "consistent with" rather than as a confirmed
    cause, and never leaks a value. The MAC bytes do not move. (docs/adr/0063,
    docs/adr/0052, karr #121, #174)
  - DOCS: the two positions a sops comment can occupy are recorded as a
    deliberate asymmetry -- a sequence-element comment is refused by
    decrypt_file/edit (YAML::XS cannot emit it), a mapping-position comment is
    dropped silently (YAML::XS discards it before parse). Both verify at sops.
    No code change. (docs/adr/0060, karr #169)
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE: a top-level `sops` data key is refused only for YAML and
    JSON, which reserve that name as their metadata root; ENV's `sops_*` keys
    and INI's `[sops]` section do not collide, so a sops-written env file
    carrying `sops=1` now round-trips end to end. No wire byte moves. (docs/adr/0061,
    karr #157)
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE: non-finite floats (.inf/-.inf/.nan) now round-trip against
    sops. In an ENCRYPTED slot they are written as type:float with plaintext
    +Inf/-Inf/NaN in both formats, as sops writes them. In an UNENCRYPTED YAML
    slot a bare non-finite float is written and read as the token sops writes;
    JSON has no spelling for one and is refused, naming the key path (sops
    refuses too). A YAML literal that overflows or underflows a double (`1e400`,
    a 400-digit integer, a bare `Inf`/`NaN`) reads back as the string sops reads
    there. A contradicting dualvar (`dualvar(+Inf,'banana')`) is still refused in
    both slots, and a bare non-finite float in an encrypted slot is derived from
    the number. edit of such a YAML document now saves instead of corrupting.
    (docs/adr/0023, docs/adr/0026, docs/adr/0031, docs/adr/0034, docs/adr/0037,
    docs/adr/0040, docs/adr/0062, karr #102, #105, #113, #114, #122, #123, #134,
    #141)
  - BUG FIX and NEW GUARD: a document nested deeper than $File::SOPS::MAX_DEPTH
    (10000 containers, sops's own limit, writable) is refused in every walk and
    both directions; deep documents no longer flood STDERR with "Deep recursion"
    warnings and are much cheaper (push/pop key path instead of a per-level
    copy). (docs/adr/0029, karr #117)
  - The remaining recursive walks (Encrypted.pm, Format/YAML.pm) are silenced of
    the residual deep-recursion warnings; no wire byte moves. (docs/adr/0057,
    karr #120, #179)
  - DOCS: policy_args records a divergence from sops on `sops.version` -- sops
    preserves a document's version across a rotate, this library stamps its own
    3.7.3. The field is unobserved here (nothing, including the MAC, reads it).
    No code change. (docs/adr/0058, karr #151)
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE: a list of `ENC[...,type:comment]` strings under an empty
    key in an INI section the rule EXCLUDES is recognised as a comment bucket
    and preserved verbatim on re-encryption, instead of being re-encrypted as
    type:str. (docs/adr/0059, karr #172)
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE: a caller string that spells `ENC[...,type:comment]` at a
    path the rule EXCLUDES is refused at write time, so this library can no
    longer produce a file it cannot read back. (docs/adr/0056, karr #168)
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE: `_path_to_aad` answers `:` for the document ROOT (an
    empty path), matching Go's `strings.Join`; no live caller reaches the empty
    case and no file's MAC moves. (docs/adr/0055, karr #156)
  - DOCS: a `type:bytes` cell this library writes crashes sops 3.13.3 (panic,
    exit 2) in every format -- a sops-side bug the library can only warn about;
    the read path is unaffected. POD warnings added. (docs/adr/0003, karr #136)
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE: a bare leading-zero YAML integer is repaired to Go's octal
    resolution on the parse path, so `0755` reads back as 493 and not 755; the
    encrypted digest does not move. (docs/adr/0054, karr #127)
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE: a value is decrypted because the document's encryption
    RULE says so, not because it looks encrypted -- decrypt asks
    should_encrypt_path about every leaf on the way out, as encrypt does on the
    way in, which is how sops reads a document. A leaf the rule EXCLUDES is a
    literal whatever its text spells (an ENC[...] string there comes back as
    that string, and the MAC covers it); a leaf the rule SELECTS that is not
    encrypted is refused at its path. Four bare shapes are left alone, as sops
    leaves them: an undef, an empty string, a comment, and an empty container.
    This REPLACES the interim guard 0.003 added for the same defect (docs/adr/0046):
    rotate and edit no longer raise "Refusing to rotate", because an excluded
    leaf is never decrypted and there is no plaintext to leak. (docs/adr/0049,
    docs/adr/0046, karr #160, #150)
  - NEW FORMAT: the INI handler (`format => 'ini'`, and any `.ini` file), a
    full round-trip against sops 3.13.3 in both directions, comments and all.
    Two-level tree (section, key), values outside a section in `DEFAULT`, Go's
    ini grammar reproduced rather than approximated. A section's comments live
    under its empty key. Four value shapes sops writes but cannot read back are
    refused, as are a nested document, a duplicate section or key, and a section
    named `sops`. (docs/adr/0047, karr #37)
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE, narrow: a mapping key `''` holding nothing but sops
    comments, below the top level, is authenticated under its PARENT's path
    rather than `parent::` -- a deliberate trade that makes the INI handler
    possible, safe because sops cannot write a sequence of only comments.
    (docs/adr/0047)
  - BUG FIX and BEHAVIOUR CHANGE: the metadata MAC's AAD is the timestamp Go
    re-formats (RFC3339), not the document's literal text, so a document
    spelling the same instant another legal way now verifies where it was
    refused. Nothing moves for a document either implementation writes (all
    carry the `Z` form). (docs/adr/0044, karr #144)
  - NEW DIAGNOSTIC: reading a YAML document whose sops `lastmodified` is a PLAIN
    (unquoted) scalar now warns -- such a file is one no sops can open. Not a
    refusal, because every write path here repairs it. (docs/adr/0050, karr #159)
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE and BUG FIX: a sops comment written as a sequence element
    (`- ENC[...,type:comment]`, in YAML and in JSON) is preserved as a
    File::SOPS::Comment leaf instead of read as an ordinary value; encrypt and
    rotate write it back. A comment is not in the MAC, in any format. Still
    refused, naming the path: a comment in a mapping-value slot, and writing a
    comment into plaintext (decrypt_file, edit). Supersedes docs/adr/0024.
    (docs/adr/0041, karr #108)
  - BUG FIX and BEHAVIOUR CHANGE: from_hash reads the non-string sops-section
    fields the way sops reads them -- `mac_only_encrypted` and `shamir_threshold`
    are decoded out of text (strconv.ParseBool / ParseInt), so a quoted
    `"false"` is boolean false (it selects the digest) and not a true Perl
    string. Unparseable spellings are refused, naming the field. Nothing moves
    for a document sops itself wrote. (docs/adr/0042, karr #138)
  - BUG FIX: from_hash refuses a LIST or a MAP in a sops-section field that
    holds a string (all nine), instead of carrying the reference into the object
    -- where an `encrypted_regex: []` made rotate write every value in plaintext,
    and a reference in `lastmodified` became the AAD. A non-string scalar and an
    unmodelled field are still passed through, now documented. (docs/adr/0043,
    karr #145, #146)
  - BUG FIX: the refusal for a YAML leaf Go resolves differently no longer
    offers an int leaf's remedy for a STRING leaf; it names the two remedies
    that work for a string (encrypt it, or write the document as JSON).
    (docs/adr/0039, karr #135)
  - The order-preserving reparse that gives MAC verification its key order is
    now a format-handler class method (`parse_in_document_order`), so a future
    ENV/INI format recovers document order instead of silently falling back to
    sorted keys; a handler that cannot recover order is a loud error. No digest
    moves. (docs/adr/0036, karr #74)
  - BUG FIX and BEHAVIOUR CHANGE: rotate/encrypt of a JSON document with a bare
    integer between 2^63 and 2^64-1 works again -- the JSON parser hands back the
    float64 Go reads (with the digits as its string half), as sops writes
    type:float there. YAML still refuses the window (sops cannot write it
    either). (docs/adr/0021, karr #101)
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE: a bare JSON integer too wide for a Perl integer is read as
    a float carrying its source spelling (sops writes type:float there), not as
    a string; a literal that overflows a double is refused on every write path.
    A quoted one is unaffected. (docs/adr/0020, karr #63)
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE: extract() returns a float that prints all its digits (a
    dualvar of the double and the wire's canonical decimal), so `"$value"` shows
    0.30000000000000004 where it showed 0.3. Only the extracted leaf is wrapped,
    and decrypt_file no longer loses those digits. (docs/adr/0010, karr #61, #79)
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE: the type:float label is kept on an encrypted whole number
    -- the read path converts with `unpack('d',pack('d',...))` instead of `+0.0`,
    so a rotate no longer relabels sops's type:float as type:int. (docs/adr/0009,
    karr #73)
  - BUG FIX: an unencrypted float needing 16-17 significant digits was written
    with 15, so the document stated one number and the MAC covered another; both
    emitters now write Go's shortest round-tripping decimal. New prerequisite
    Math::BigFloat. (docs/adr/0006, karr #58)
  - BUG FIX: a negative zero keeps its sign -- an unencrypted YAML `-0.0` is
    written `-0.0` (the spelling both implementations read as the double), an
    encrypted `-0` keeps its sign on the read path, and a YAML negative zero
    written as JSON is carried by the double. (docs/adr/0006, docs/adr/0014,
    karr #62, #72, #88)
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE: an integer leaf carrying a different string half (a
    dualvar, or a YAML source spelling) is refused at emit time in both formats,
    instead of writing a document that fails its own MAC. (docs/adr/0012, karr #84)
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE and fix: a float leaf carrying its own string form reached
    a JSON document as a quoted string; it is now written as the canonical
    decimal bare number, as YAML always did. (docs/adr/0011, karr #78)
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE: Perl's native boolean SV (`builtin::is_bool` -- `!!1`, a
    comparison, `defined`, ...) is written as type:bool True/False, as sops
    writes a boolean, instead of as the integer 1/0. Wire bytes move for a
    previously-written encrypted `!!1`; the read path is unchanged. (docs/adr/0016,
    karr #90)
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE: a negative zero on which Perl cached an integer is written
    type:float `-0` (what sops writes), decided on the NV's bit pattern, instead
    of the integer 0. (docs/adr/0015, karr #89)
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE: a YAML leaf whose spelling Go resolves differently from
    libyaml (`0755`, `010`, `0x1f`, `1_000`, `.inf`, a bare timestamp, ...) is
    refused when a MAC-carrying document is written, naming the key path -- the
    file used to be written silently and rejected later with a MAC mismatch.
    Encrypted slots, JSON and the plaintext emitters are untouched. (docs/adr/0013,
    karr #86)
  - internal: the YAML foreign-resolution guard takes its verdict from the token
    the emitter writes, fixing the one leaf class (a boolean) where the leaf's
    stringification and the emitted token differ. No document moves. (docs/adr/0017,
    karr #91)
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE, diagnostics only: encrypting a YAML document with a `True`
    or `False` STRING in an unencrypted slot warns -- libyaml writes it bare and
    Go reads a boolean, a type divergence a sops write-back would make permanent.
    No document stops being written. (docs/adr/0019, karr #92)
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE, diagnostics only: encrypting a YAML document with
    mac_only_encrypted set warns about an unencrypted leaf whose spelling sops
    resolves differently (e.g. `0755` -> 493). No document moves. (docs/adr/0018,
    karr #87)
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE: a YAML or JSON document with a REFERENCE as a leaf value
    is refused instead of written -- a blessed or unblessed ref digests as its
    stringification while the emitter writes a Perl-specific tag or a heap
    address, so the file was unreadable. An exact JSON::PP::Boolean is still
    written as bare true/false; encrypted slots are unaffected. An unblessed ref
    in an encrypted slot is refused too, rather than stored as a heap address.
    (docs/adr/0008, karr #65, #66, #67)
  - SECURITY: the random data key (Backend::Age) and the generated data key and
    per-value GCM nonce (Encrypted) are checked for length where they are
    produced, and there is one generator left; a short return (a valid but wrong
    AES key, or a repeatable nonce) now dies instead of reaching the cipher. No
    correctly generated document is affected. (karr #52)
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE: a value's type comes from the value, not from a pattern
    match on its text -- a quoted "false"/"1"/"007" is type:str, a bare false is
    type:bool, and a Perl number is written in Go's canonical form. type:bool now
    requires JSON->true/false or a boolean loaded from YAML/JSON. Existing
    encrypted documents are unaffected. (docs/adr/0002)
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE: values are UTF-8 encoded unconditionally, like the AAD,
    instead of only when the scalar carries Perl's UTF-8 flag -- a Latin-1 value
    used to reach the wire as Latin-1 and be read by sops as binary. Passing
    UTF-8 bytes to encrypt now double-encodes; decode at your boundary first.
    type:bytes is exempt. (docs/adr/0003)
  - New File::SOPS::Encrypted class methods detect_type and value_to_bytes, the
    single source of truth for the value-to-wire mapping, which previously
    existed twice (ciphertext and MAC digest) and had to be kept identical by
    hand.
  - SECURITY: decrypt no longer returns data it could not verify -- a missing
    mac, a malformed mac, and a mac that will not decrypt all used to silently
    disable verification and return the document as though checked; all three
    now die, matching sops. Pass ignore_mac => 1 for the old, unauthenticated
    behaviour.
  - Fix MAC verification of any document with a value excluded from encryption:
    unencrypted values are covered on both sides now, where they were hashed on
    encrypt and skipped on decrypt, so the default _unencrypted suffix made the
    library reject its own output.
  - Fix decrypt being non-deterministic: a type:float came back an order of
    magnitude out on about one leaf in a hundred, because CryptX returns a
    plaintext buffer that is not NUL-terminated and Perl's string-to-number
    conversion ran off the end. The plaintext is normalised in decrypt_bytes.
    (t/18)
  - MAC verification recovers document key order from an order-preserving
    reparse (YAML::PP, new prerequisite) instead of scraping ENC values out of
    the raw text, which is what makes unencrypted values placeable at all.
  - Fix any document with a non-ASCII key: the AAD is now always UTF-8, matching
    sops and our own emitters, where a key above U+00FF died and a Latin-1 key
    was authenticated as Latin-1. Affects reading, writing and the MAC.
  - Fix decrypted values coming back as UTF-8 bytes where encrypt took
    characters; decrypt, decrypt_file and extract now return characters. The
    bytes written to the file are unchanged.
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE: the top-level `sops` key is reserved -- encrypt refuses a
    data structure carrying it (the metadata would overwrite the value), and
    encrypt_file refuses an already-encrypted input (a second encryption under a
    new data key made the values unrecoverable). A non-mapping `sops` entry is
    covered by the same rule instead of being deleted silently. Use rotate to
    re-key.
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE: an integer outside Go's int64 range is refused rather than
    written (no wire form preserves it in either format), naming the key path;
    store the digits as a string to keep them. Reading a value sops legitimately
    truncated is unaffected.
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE: an unknown `type:` field is an error instead of quietly
    returning the raw plaintext; type:time and type:comment are added to the
    ladder, and a type:float plaintext that is not a number is an error too.
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE: base64 in an ENC value is decoded strictly and names the
    field, where MIME::Base64 silently ignored corruption and surfaced it much
    later. is_encrypted still never dies.
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE: encrypt_value refuses an empty value (GCM ciphertext is as
    long as its plaintext, so an empty one is a string neither this module nor
    sops accepts); sops leaves empty values unencrypted, as encrypt does.
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE: extract dies on a path that does not exist, naming the
    missing component, where a missing top-level key used to return undef;
    bracket notation with a bare array index or a single-quoted key now works,
    as in `sops --extract`.
  - Fix undef being written as an empty string, and $YAML::XS::Boolean being set
    process-wide at load time (it is now `local` around this distribution's own
    calls).
  - Failures say where and why: value errors name the key path on both sides, a
    structural disagreement is reported at its path instead of truncating the
    digest, and the MAC message says how many leaves were covered and how to
    read the file anyway. No message contains a plaintext value, a data key or
    an identity.
  - Fix multi-document YAML being silently reduced to its last document (data
    loss on write); such input is now refused, because sops's whole-stream model
    is a data-model change not in this release. Reading a multi-document sops
    file now says so instead of reporting a MAC mismatch.
  - Fix the encryption rules being applied one level at a time instead of to a
    value's whole key path, which left everything under an `api_enc:` block
    readable and produced a file sops refused. Latent until the rules became
    configurable.
  - SECURITY: rotate no longer silently discards the parts of the sops section
    it did not create (rules, mac_only_encrypted, and every backend's key
    material but age), which revoked PGP/KMS recipients while reporting success;
    it now carries them across and REFUSES a file holding key material it cannot
    re-encrypt rather than dropping it. rotate also dies with a clear message on
    a file with no sops section.
  - The four encryption rules (unencrypted_suffix, encrypted_suffix,
    unencrypted_regex, encrypted_regex), documented since 0.001, are reachable
    at last as arguments to encrypt and encrypt_file, or carried over with
    metadata => $meta. Setting two mutually-exclusive rules dies here instead of
    producing a file sops refuses.
  - Metadata preserves the sops-section fields it does not model
    (shamir_threshold, key_groups, and anything a later sops adds) verbatim
    across a rewrite, in a new `extra` attribute; new methods policy_args and
    rule_value.
  - New method encrypt_in_place (encrypt_file over a single file), with an
    atomic write, preserved permissions, and an already-encrypted file refused
    as encrypt_file refuses it.
  - New method edit: decrypt, open $EDITOR, re-encrypt. The plaintext goes to a
    0600 file in a 0700 temp directory removed on every exit path; returns 0
    when nothing changed, dies with the file untouched on editor failure, a
    parse error, or a top-level sops entry. Two deliberate differences from sops
    edit: no editor fallback when EDITOR is unset, and re-encryption under a NEW
    data key (so edit refuses the same files rotate does). A reserved-key entry
    and an unparseable document are reported apart. (karr #41)
  - New method creation_rules_for: reads the .sops.yaml governing a file and
    returns the encrypt arguments its first matching creation rule asks for, so
    encrypt_in_place can encrypt a file the way its repository has decided.
    path_regex is matched as sops matches it (relative to the config dir). ONE
    DELIBERATE DIFFERENCE: the search walks up from the FILE, not the working
    directory, and $SOPS_CONFIG is not read from the environment. A rule naming
    key material this distribution cannot produce is refused rather than
    half-applied. (karr #45, #53, #54)
  - DATA LOSS: encrypt_file, decrypt_file and rotate write their output
    atomically, as encrypt_in_place and edit already did -- all three used `>`,
    which truncated the target before writing and checked neither print nor
    close, so a failed write left an EMPTY file and still returned true. A
    consequence: a written file comes back with a NEW INODE (hard links keep the
    old content), and replacing a file needs write permission on its directory.
    (docs/adr for file writing; karr #46)
  - New method emit on both format handlers, so decrypt_file and edit write
    their plaintext through the same code serialize does -- the second emitter
    they each carried had to stay byte-identical by hand and nothing checked it.
    (t/20)
  - Fix JSON output and parsing depending on which JSON backend the calling
    program loaded: Format::JSON now names Cpanel::JSON::XS (new direct
    prerequisite) for both. Two defects go with it, reachable only under
    JSON::XS-first: a sops-written unencrypted float refused with a MAC mismatch
    (JSON::XS is not correctly rounded), and an unencrypted `-0` failing its own
    MAC. Duplicate JSON keys are refused. (docs/adr/0005)
  - Tests: the interop suite is no longer the only compatibility coverage. New
    regression tests pin the wire format, key ordering, every MAC rule and the
    encoding boundary without a sops binary, including a checked-in document the
    real sops 3.13.3 wrote.
  - BUG FIX: a document that contains itself is refused, naming the path where
    the cycle closes, instead of hanging the process; a merely reused anchor is
    unaffected. An acyclic but exponentially-shared document is refused
    separately, at go-yaml's own aliasing budget. (docs/adr/0025, docs/adr/0027,
    karr #110, #112)
  - NEW: a plaintext YAML tagging a scalar `!!bool true`/`false` is encrypted
    instead of refused (that tag carries no type and is dropped before parsing);
    every other yaml.org scalar tag carries a TYPE and is refused by name, with
    a message about the document rather than about libyaml. sops's own message
    for the unopenable file is replaced. (docs/adr/0032, karr #118)
  - DECISION, no behaviour change: the per-format type policy the ENV and INI
    handlers were waiting on is settled -- our emitters write value_to_bytes
    verbatim into an unencrypted flat slot, so the digest does not move and each
    line is one sops itself writes; the metadata half is decoded weakly in
    from_hash. docs/adr/0002 is extended, not changed. (docs/adr/0035, karr #77)
  - NEW FORMAT: `format => 'env'` (dotenv), a `.env` document through every
    method, auto-detected, proved both ways against sops 3.13.3. A comment lives
    under the EMPTY KEY as a list of File::SOPS::Comment objects (measured: every
    comment authenticates under the AAD `:`), so a .env keeps its comments
    through decrypt_file and edit. Keys are written sorted (the MAC's encrypt
    side hashes sort keys); reading takes document order from the reparse. Ten
    shapes are refused, each naming the line or key. (docs/adr/0045, karr #36)

  IMPORTANT -- if you encrypted data with 0.001 or 0.002, read this. The defects
  below could corrupt or misrepresent stored values, and some are not repairable
  from the ciphertext:
    * A false boolean was written as an unencrypted empty value; such a document
      also fails its own MAC, so this release may refuse it, and the original
      value is gone.
    * The string 'false' was encrypted as True -- a silent value inversion that
      decrypts without error.
    * No YAML file written by 0.001/0.002 could be read by the sops CLI; those
      files are readable again once re-encrypted with this version.
    * A key in the Latin-1 range was written with a Latin-1 AAD where sops uses
      UTF-8; neither sops nor this release can decrypt it, and keys above U+00FF
      died. A Latin-1 VALUE passed as an unflagged string was written as Latin-1;
      it still verifies but comes back as bytes -- re-encrypting fixes it.
    * encrypt_file on an already-encrypted file re-encrypted every value under a
      NEW data key and wrote over the original: THE VALUES ARE GONE unless you
      have the earlier copy, whose sops section holds the missing key.
    * A top-level key literally named 'sops' was overwritten by the metadata; a
      null value was written as an empty string. Both repairable from plaintext.
    * READING was affected regardless of who wrote the file: decrypt could hand
      back a type:float an order of magnitude out (100000000000000000000 as
      1e21), non-reproducibly. The stored files are intact and read correctly
      now; anything DERIVED from a value read with 0.001/0.002 may be wrong.
  Re-encrypt affected documents from their ORIGINAL PLAINTEXT, not from the
  stored ciphertext. Where a value gated access, verify it by hand.

0.002     2026-02-22 02:10:34Z
  - Fix bool deserialization: return JSON::PP::Boolean instead of plain 1/0
    (YAML::XS with $Boolean='JSON::PP' now correctly serializes true/false)

0.001     2026-01-11 04:00:35Z
  - Initial release
  - Pure Perl implementation of Mozilla SOPS encrypted file format
  - Full interoperability with sops CLI (tested with v3.9.4)
  - age encryption backend via Crypt::Age
  - AES-256-GCM value encryption with type preservation (str, int, float, bool)
  - YAML and JSON format support
  - MAC verification for tamper detection
  - Multiple recipient support
  - Key rotation support
  - File encrypt/decrypt operations
  - Single value extraction via path notation
  - Comprehensive test suite including CLI interop tests
